This study introduces an extension of the restricted signed sumset concept (denoted $k\hat{A}$) applicable to finite sets of real numbers, where each element may be combined with a sign at most $k$ times. We establish inequalities bounding the size of $k\hat{A}$ based on the cardinality of $A$, demonstrating that while these lower limits generally increase as $k$ rises within the range $1 \le k \le |A|$, they converge to $2|A|$ as $k$ approaches this upper limit. The initial investigation identifies integer sets producing the minimal sets under this extension, with findings indicating that for fixed set sizes greater than two, such minimal sets invariably share the structure of arithmetic progressions with the smallest possible common difference. Subsequent analysis of sets with rank 3 (where rank is defined by the common difference of their arithmetic progression structure) explores parameters minimizing the $k\hat{A}$ set magnitude and proves a conjecture regarding the long-standing lower limit for Pascal triangle rows: specifically, that the restricted partial sums set $\binom{k}{A}$ has a cardinality of at least $2k+2$.

